/* book Worked example 2: Q: For the R = 4 Ω case of the first example (L = 1 H, C = 0.01 F, 10 V step, nothing stored), find vC(t), the time of the first peak and the peak voltage.
   1. Underdamped with α = 2 s⁻¹ and ωd = 9.80 rad/s, so vC(t) = 10 + e−2t(A₁ cos 9.80t + A₂ sin 9.80t).
   2. vC(0⁺) = 0: 10 + A₁ = 0, so A₁ = −10.
   3. iL(0⁺) = 0 and i = C dvC/dt, so dvC/dt(0⁺) = 0. Differentiating at t = 0: −αA₁ + ωd A₂ = 0, so A₂ = αA₁/ωd = (2 × −10)/9.80 = −2.04.
   4. vC(t) = 10 − e−2t(10 cos 9.80t + 2.04 sin 9.80t) V. Check: vC(0) = 10 − 10 = 0 ✓ and vC(∞) = 10 V ✓.
   5. The first peak comes at ωd t = π: t = π/9.80 = 0.321 s. There cos = −1 and sin = 0, so vC = 10 + 10 e−0.641 = 10 + 5.27 = 15.27 V.
   Answer: vC(t) = 10 − e−2t(10 cos 9.80t + 2.04 sin 9.80t) V; the first peak is 15.27 V at t = 0.321 s, an overshoot of 52.7 %. */
