/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Write the source as vs = Vm sin(ωt + φ).
   - Inductor current cannot jump.
   - Series RC works the same way.
   - i(t) = Im sin(ωt + φ − θ) + K e−t/τ, K = i(0⁺) − Im sin(φ − θ)
   - Im = Vm/|Z| is peak steady current in A; θ is the impedance angle; τ = L/R is the time constant in s; K is the initial offset in A.
   - At the switch, the sine part has some value.
   - The largest offset occurs when iss(0) is at a peak, ±Im.
   - After 5τ the exponential is below 1 % of K and is treated as gone; in the example below τ = 0.25 s, so the transient is over in about 1.25 s.
   - An RLC circuit under AC behaves in the same pattern: the steady state is still a phasor, and the transient is a second-order solution from second-order RLC circuits.
   - In the figure, at t = 0 the steady sine is 2 sin(−0.644) = −1.2 A.
*/
