/* book exam traps: Finding the phasor steady-state current and stopping there. The examiner wants the exponential term and K. | Using K = i(0⁺) − 0 instead of K = i(0⁺) − iss(0). The sine part is not zero at switching in general. | Wrong sign of θ. In RL the steady current lags, so write φ − θ. In RC the current leads, so write φ + θ. | Swapping time constants: τ = L/R for RL and τ = RC for RC. R in ohms, L in henry, C in farad give seconds. In RC, find i(0⁺) from KVL: capacitor voltage cannot jump, but current can. | Assuming the source peak is always the worst switching instant. For a purely inductive circuit the worst instant is the source zero crossing; compare iss(0) with i(0⁺) instead. */
